<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Murad II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1404">1404</a>–<a href="/years/1451">1451</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-dynasty">Ottoman dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Murad II was sultan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> from 1421 to 1451, with a brief abdication in 1444 to 1446. He restored Ottoman power after the civil war that followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s victory, inherited the Germiyanid lands in 1429, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1430, and defeated crusading armies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">Varna</a> in 1444 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> in 1448. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/murad-ii.jpg" alt="Murad II" width="320" height="498" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 1584-1588 artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Murad_II._H%C3%BCnername,_folio_143b._TSMK_Hazine_1523_(created_1584-1588).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Murad was born in June 1404 to Mehmed I. The identity of his mother is disputed: a 15th-century historian called her a concubine, a 20th-century writer named Şahzade Hatun, daughter of Divitdar Ahmed Pasha, and the most widely accepted view is that she was Emine Hatun of the Dulkadirid Beylik. He spent his early childhood in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasya">Amasya</a>, came to the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Edirne</a> with his father in 1410, and was made governor of the Amasya Sanjak after Mehmed became sultan. When Mehmed I died in 1421 Murad was recognised as sultan at 16 and girded with the Sword of Osman at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bursa">Bursa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His reign was troubled by insurrection from the start. The Byzantine Emperor Manuel II released a pretender, Düzmece Mustafa, who defeated Murad's general Bayazid Pasha and declared himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Adrianople. Murad out-manoeuvred him, his troops defected, and with help from a Genoese commander named Adorno he besieged and stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a>, where Mustafa was taken and put to death. Murad then formed a new army, the Azeb, and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, but had to abandon the siege when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, in league with Anatolian states, sent his 13-year-old brother Küçük Mustafa to rebel and besiege <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bursa">Bursa</a>. Murad executed him and annexed the Aydinid, Germiyanid, Menteshe and Teke states.</p><p>He defeated the Karamanids in 1428, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> withdrew in 1432 after Murad took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> in 1430. In the 1430s he captured large territories in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> in 1439. He won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade-of-varna">Crusade of Varna</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hunyadi">John Hunyadi</a> in 1444, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">Varna</a>, according to the chronicles, he dismounted to pray as the Hungarians gained ground, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-iii-of-poland">Władysław III of Poland</a>, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, was killed in a charge. Exhausted by war and by the death of his son Shehzade Alaedin, he signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-szeged">Peace of Szeged</a> and abdicated in favour of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a>, then 12. A rebellion at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Edirne</a> in 1446, probably sparked by debasement of the akçe, brought about Murad's return to the throne.</p><p>He presented himself as a ghazi sultan, modelling his conduct on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> of the epic Battalname, and a collection of his campaigns was given the title The Ghazas of Sultan Murad. His reign saw an increase in trade and the growth of cities; in 1432 the traveller Bertrandon de la Broquière noted that Ottoman annual revenue had risen to 2,500,000 ducats.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Murad II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His origins are uncertain: the historian Ferishta repeats a story that he was a son of the Ottoman sultan <b>Murad II</b>, but he was more probably a Turkman or Persian who came to the Deccan as a young man and rose in Bahmani service.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Michael I beat the Ottomans in Severin in 1418–1420 and was killed in the counter-offensive; Dan II defeated <b>Murad II</b> in 1422 with Pippo Spano's help.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-wallachia">Principality of Wallachia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The death of Sultan <b>Murad II</b> brought his 18-year-old son Mehmed II back to power.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-3">3 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Ottoman Sultan <b>Murad II</b> laid siege to Constantinople, the Byzantine capital, after Emperor Manuel II had tried to interfere in the Ottoman succession following Mehmed I's death.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-10">10 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After a siege begun in 1422, the Ottoman Empire under Sultan <b>Murad II</b> captured Thessaloniki from the Republic of Venice.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-29">29 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>An Ottoman army led by Sultan <b>Murad II</b> and his crown prince Mehmed II besieged Krujë, the Albanian capital of Skanderbeg's League of Lezhë.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-14">14 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasya">Amasya</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Edirne</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.65,35.83&amp;year=1434" aria-label="Amasya on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5429 712 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Murad II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5879 982h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5879 982h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.65,35.83&amp;year=1434">What was here in 1434? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
